.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt UNHARE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm unhare
.Nd extract the filesystem bundled into a Bun standalone binary
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl flv
.Op Fl o Ar pattern
.Op Fl s Ar section
.Ar file
.Nm
.Fl t
.Op Fl flv
.Op Fl o Ar pattern
.Op Fl s Ar section
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility unpacks the modules that
.Xr bun 1
embeds into an executable produced by
.Cm bun build Fl Fl compile ,
writing each of them out as a regular file.
.Pp
The bundle is located by asking
.Xr elf 3
for the
.Dq Li .bun
section of
.Ar file ,
so a payload that is not the last thing in the file
.Pq one followed by a section header table, a symbol table or debugging information
is found just as reliably as one appended to the end.
Section names are not unique in ELF, so every section called
.Dq Li .bun
is gathered, and each bundle among them unpacked in turn.
Module names are a bundle's own business and nothing keeps them apart
across bundles, so each is given a directory of its own, named by the
.Fl o
pattern.
Should no such section hold one, as happens with a stripped executable,
with the older
.Xr bun 1
releases that merely concatenated the payload,
or with a Mach-O or PE binary built for another platform,
.Nm
scans the
whole file for the bundle trailer instead.
The trailer search knows nothing of ELF, so a binary of any format
unpacks so long as the bundle is in it somewhere.
.Pp
Should
.Xr elf_begin 3
itself fail, which takes an exhausted system
rather than an unusual file,
.Nm
says so and stops.
It does not scan in that case: the bytes it would scan are the ones
libelf could not get hold of.
.Pp
Module names are taken from Bun's virtual filesystem, whose
.Pa /$bunfs/
and
.Pa B:\e~BUN\e
prefixes are stripped before the name is appended to the output directory.
A name that would place a file outside that directory is refused and the
module is skipped with a warning.
.Pp
An existing file is never replaced unless
.Fl f
is given.
.Pp
Neither is a symbolic link followed, with
.Fl f
or without it, whether
it stands for the file itself or for a directory along the way.
A link left in the output directory would otherwise carry the write
outside it, which is the very thing refusing
.Pa ..
in a name is meant
to prevent.
The directory named by
.Fl o
is the user's own and is exempt; only what
lies beneath it is held to this.
.Pp
Names are printed with their control characters escaped, as
.Ql \e033
and the like.
A name out of the bundle is not to be trusted with a terminal: an escape
sequence among them can rewrite what is shown, and a newline can forge
an entry in a listing meant to be read a line at a time.
Bytes at or above 0x80 are left alone, so UTF-8 names stay legible.
.Pp
Each module records the encoding of its contents.
Text that does not fit in Latin-1 is bundled as UTF-16LE, and
.Nm
converts it back to UTF-8 on the way out, so that the extracted file
reads the way the original source did.
Contents recorded as Latin-1 or as binary are written through unchanged.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl f
Overwrite files that are already there.
Without it,
.Nm
writes nothing at all when any of the files it would
create exists already, so that a run which would clobber something
leaves the directory as it found it rather than stopping partway
through with the job half done.
.It Fl h
Print the usage to standard output and exit successfully.
.It Fl l
List the files that would be written, one to a line, and write
none of them.
Nothing is created, not even the output directory, and an existing file
in the way is neither an error nor worth remarking on, there being
nothing to overwrite.
The names are the ones a real run would produce, output directory and
all, so that what is listed is what would appear.
A single
.Fl v
annotates them, the listing being the point already.
.It Fl o Ar pattern
Write the extracted files below the directory
.Ar pattern
names, creating it and any missing parents if necessary.
The default is the current directory.
.Pp
A single
.Xr printf 3
conversion in
.Ar pattern
is replaced by the
index of the section a bundle came from, so that
.Fl o Li out%u
unpacks the bundle in section 29 into
.Pa out29 .
Only the integer conversions are allowed, with their flags and widths;
anything else is refused rather than handed on to
.Xr printf 3 .
A bundle found by scanning, rather than in a section, answers to zero.
When more than one bundle is to be unpacked a pattern without a
conversion is an error, naming as it does a single place for all of
them.
.It Fl s Ar section
Unpack only the bundle in the given section, which
may be given more than once to name several.
The default is to unpack every one of them.
.It Fl t
Unpack the bundle built into
.Nm
itself, rather than one
named on the command line, and take no
.Ar file
operand.
The program to read is the one named by
.Va argv Ns Bq 0 ,
or, when that names no readable file, the one the kernel reports for
this process.
The bundle holds the
.Pa tests
directory of the distribution, which
.Cm make test
unpacks and compares against the original.
.It Fl v
Be verbose.
Given once, write the name of each extracted file to standard output,
one to a line, and report on standard error where the bundle was found
and how much was written.
Given twice, annotate each name with the number of bytes written, the
loader, module format, encoding and side, and report the bundle header
as well.
.Pp
The listing goes to standard output and everything else to standard
error, so that a single
.Fl v
leaves a list of names fit to pipe
onward.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success.
A failure exits with whichever code from
.In sysexits.h
fits it:
.Bl -tag -width EX_CANTCREAT -compact -offset indent
.It Dv EX_USAGE
An unknown option, or the wrong number of operands.
.It Dv EX_NOINPUT
The named file cannot be read at all.
.It Dv EX_DATAERR
It can, but holds no bundle that can be made sense of.
.It Dv EX_CANTCREAT
The output cannot be laid down, an existing file in
the way among the reasons.
.It Dv EX_IOERR
Writing the output failed partway through.
.It Dv EX_OSERR
The system denied us memory, or an answer it was asked
for.
.It Dv EX_SOFTWARE
The ELF library is too old to talk to.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Unpack an application into
.Pa ./app-files ,
listing what is written:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unhare -v -o app-files ./app
.Ed
.Pp
See what would be written, and where, without writing it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unhare -l -o app-files ./app
.Ed
.Pp
Unpack a binary carrying more than one bundle, each below the
number of the section it came from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unhare -o 'app%u' ./app
.Ed
.Pp
Unpack only the bundle in section 29:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unhare -s 29 -o app-files ./app
.Ed
.Pp
Unpack the bundle this program carries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unhare -t -o /tmp/unhare-tests
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Nm
carries a bundle of its own, in its own
.Dq Li .bun
section, so that
.Cm make test
can check a freshly built program against a known answer.
Running
.Nm
on its own executable, with or without
.Fl t ,
therefore unpacks that bundle rather than reporting that nothing was
found.
.Pp
Each directory of the bundled tree is stored under the Bun encoding it
is named for, so that one tree exercises all three:
.Pa US-ASCII
as Latin-1,
.Pa UTF-16
as UTF-16LE, and anything else
as binary, which is what a legacy eight-bit encoding such as
.Pa KOI8-U
needs if its bytes are to survive the round trip.
.Pp
The files under
.Pa UTF-16
are themselves UTF-8 on disk.
The conversion happens on the way into the bundle and is undone on the
way out, so the round trip returns them unchanged; a file already in
UTF-16 could not round-trip, this program's business being to hand back
decoded UTF-8.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
The encoding recorded as value 2 in a module entry holds UTF-16LE code
units, not UTF-8;
.Nm
reports it as
.Dq Li utf16 .
Tools that follow an older description of this format name it
.Dq Li utf8
and write its contents out verbatim, which yields text files no other
program can read.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr elf 3 ,
.Xr elf_getshdrstrndx 3 ,
.Xr gelf_getshdr 3 ,
.Xr printf 3
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Mikhail Teterin
.Sh CAVEATS
Bytecode, module metadata and source maps stored alongside a module are
reported under
.Fl v
but are not written out;
only the module contents are extracted.
